# Append to ~/.ssh/config on L0, adjusting IdentityFile.
#
# All connections are initiated from L0 inwards; nothing downstream may
# initiate outwards. No forced command and no restrict on the key: both would
# break ProxyJump, the credential push and vncviewer -via.

Host l1
    HostName 10.99.0.2
    User root
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/agent-stack
    IdentitiesOnly yes

# Sandboxes, by internal name only. The zone must match site.nix's
# internalDomain; this file is plain text and cannot read it. Deliberately not by address: a pattern
# like 10.42.* would match any host on any network you ever reach, and L1's
# internal addressing does not belong in your personal ssh config.
#
# Nothing on L0 needs to resolve these names. With ProxyJump the destination
# is resolved by the jump host, and L1's unbound is authoritative for the zone.
#
# Host key checking is off here, and only here. A sandbox regenerates its host
# keys on every `agentctl reset`, which is the point of a reset, so strict
# checking would mean clearing a stale entry after each one — and a habit of
# dismissing host key warnings costs more than this check is worth. The check
# that matters is on l1 above: reaching a sandbox means authenticating to L1
# first, and anything able to forge a sandbox key from inside L1's tap already
# owns L1. Sending these to /dev/null keeps the real known_hosts meaningful.
Host *.agents.contained
    ProxyJump l1
    User agent
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/agent-stack
    IdentitiesOnly yes
    UserKnownHostsFile /dev/null
    StrictHostKeyChecking no
    LogLevel ERROR
